\ A flagged quotation body that ends in a self-tail-call leaves through the back-edge, which jumps
\ to the compiled word's own loop header rather than returning. The splice's epilogue pop never
\ runs on that route, and the compiled-entry boundary only truncates once the word returns, so the
\ transient frames would pile up for the whole loop.
\
\ The memory cap in the flags sidecar is the discriminator: 20000 leaked frames blow it, while a
\ balanced bracket keeps the run flat. There is no AOT redirect, because a binary has no way to
\ set the cap and the default one is too loose to catch the leak.

countdown: ( n -- n ) [
  dup 0 >
  [ helper: [ 1 ] ; 1 - countdown ]
  [ ]
  if
] ;

20000 countdown drop

"done" print-line
